      <section>
        <h2>What Khan Academy does well</h2>
        <p>Khan Academy teaches. Videos, worked lessons, a structured path through every SAT Math topic, and progress tracking, all free from a nonprofit. It is also the College Board's official practice partner, which gives its SAT material an alignment nobody else can claim.</p>
        <p>If a concept is missing, a lesson platform is the right tool for the job. No amount of drilling fixes not knowing how exponent rules work; a ten minute video does.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What Satified does differently</h2>
        <p>Satified is not a course and does not pretend to be one. It is 1,483 question generators organized by the digital SAT's own taxonomy of 4 domains and 19 skills, tiered easy, medium, and hard, plus adaptive practice tests that copy the real two module structure with built in Desmos. Every answer, figure, and explanation has been independently verified.</p>
        <p>A common complaint about practice tools in general is that their questions feel gentler than the real thing. Satified is calibrated hardest where it counts, at the medium and hard tiers where scores actually move. And because every question regenerates with fresh numbers, the bank never runs out and never gets memorized.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The honest answer is both</h2>
        <p>Learn on Khan Academy, drill on Satified. When a lesson clicks, come drill the matching skill until the generator stops surprising you, then go learn the next thing. The two loops feed each other, and since both are free, the usual tradeoff between tools simply does not exist here.</p>
        <p>The only students who can treat Satified as a full alternative are the ones who already know the concepts and are purely short on volume: they need reps at real difficulty, not another explanation of slope.</p>
